==============================================================================
 [4] Connection limit: total cap + per-client-address cap
==============================================================================
Scenario: 20 idle clients from 127.0.0.1 flood the server, then an ordinary
          client connects from 127.0.0.2 (all of 127.0.0.0/8 is this host).

--- WITHOUT: ./server --no-limit ---
$ ./server -p 9090 --no-limit
$ ./client --mode idle --hold 3   x 20 from 127.0.0.1 (one host floods the server)
server children 1 s later: 20
$ ./client --bind 127.0.0.2 -q   (an ordinary client from another address)
  seq=0   pid=26442  host=LAPTOP-0F0NVT4H up=0d 05:11:43 load=0.00/0.01/0.02 mem_avail=7.06 GiB/7.61 GiB
server log:
flood children: 20 | flood clients refused (ERR_BUSY): 0/20 | 127.0.0.2: served

--- CAP: ./server -m 10 ---
$ ./server -p 9090 -m 10
$ ./client --mode idle --hold 3   x 20 from 127.0.0.1 (one host floods the server)
server children 1 s later: 10
$ ./client --bind 127.0.0.2 -q   (an ordinary client from another address)
  23:35:22.604 [client:26515] ERROR server error 6: server busy: 10/10 connections in use
server log:
  23:35:21.603 [server:26473] WARN  rejected 127.0.0.1:34190: server busy: 10/10 connections in use (no fork)
  23:35:21.604 [server:26473] WARN  rejected 127.0.0.1:34210: server busy: 10/10 connections in use (no fork)
flood children: 10 | flood clients refused (ERR_BUSY): 10/20 | 127.0.0.2: refused

--- WITH: ./server -m 10 --max-per-ip 5 ---
$ ./server -p 9090 -m 10 --max-per-ip 5
$ ./client --mode idle --hold 3   x 20 from 127.0.0.1 (one host floods the server)
server children 1 s later: 5
$ ./client --bind 127.0.0.2 -q   (an ordinary client from another address)
  seq=0   pid=26584  host=LAPTOP-0F0NVT4H up=0d 05:11:49 load=0.00/0.01/0.02 mem_avail=7.06 GiB/7.61 GiB
server log:
  23:35:24.924 [server:26546] WARN  rejected 127.0.0.1:34296: too many connections from your address (5/5) (no fork)
  23:35:24.925 [server:26546] WARN  rejected 127.0.0.1:34310: too many connections from your address (5/5) (no fork)
flood children: 5 | flood clients refused (ERR_BUSY): 15/20 | 127.0.0.2: served

--- Checks [4] ---
  [PASS] without: one host gets 20 children, no bound
  [PASS] total cap only: 10 children, but 127.0.0.2 is starved
  [PASS] with per-IP cap: 5 children, 15 flood clients refused
  [PASS] with per-IP cap: 127.0.0.2 is still served

==============================================================================
 [5] Slow sender: whole-request deadline vs per-read timeout
==============================================================================
Scenario: a 16-byte request sent one byte every 3 s. Each read() gets its
          byte before the 5 s SO_RCVTIMEO, so only a deadline can stop it.

--- WITHOUT ---
$ ./server -p 9090 --no-deadline
$ ./client --mode slow --hold 9   (16-byte request, 1 byte every 3 s)
6.5 s after the first byte, server children:
    PID    PPID STAT    VSZ   RSS     ELAPSED CMD
  26624   26615 S+     2820   928       00:06 ./server -p 9090 --no-deadline
client output:
  23:35:28.251 [client:26622] INFO  slow sender: a 16-byte request, one byte every 3000 ms (45 s for all of it)
  RESULT: no reaction within 9 s - 3/16 bytes sent, the server child is still waiting for the rest
server log:
  23:35:28.139 [server:26615] INFO  robustness: [4] connection limit 128 total, off per IP | [5] request deadline OFF | [6] stream framing ON | [7] heartbeat ON | [9] die with parent ON
  23:35:37.264 [server:26615] INFO  reaped child 26624: exit 0 (client closed normally) | active=0

--- WITH ---
$ ./server -p 9090 
$ ./client --mode slow --hold 9   (16-byte request, 1 byte every 3 s)
6.5 s after the first byte, server children:
    PID    PPID STAT    VSZ   RSS     ELAPSED CMD
  (no child processes)
client output:
  23:35:37.405 [client:26640] INFO  slow sender: a 16-byte request, one byte every 3000 ms (45 s for all of it)
  (the server reacted after 2 of 16 bytes)
  RESULT: server rejected the request after 5.00 s: error 8 (request deadline exceeded)
server log:
  23:35:37.290 [server:26633] INFO  robustness: [4] connection limit 128 total, off per IP | [5] request deadline 5s | [6] stream framing ON | [7] heartbeat ON | [9] die with parent ON
  23:35:42.408 [server:26642] WARN  127.0.0.1:49536: request still incomplete 5 s after its first byte -> slow sender cut off (deadline)
  23:35:42.410 [server:26633] INFO  reaped child 26642: exit 6 (request deadline exceeded, slow sender cut off) | active=0

--- Checks [5] ---
  [PASS] without: the child is still waiting after 6.5 s
  [PASS] with: the child cut the sender off (error 8) and exited

==============================================================================
 [6] TCP is a byte stream: split and glued messages
==============================================================================
Scenario: fragment = one request in 8 one-byte segments, 100 ms apart
          pipeline = 5 requests glued into one write()

--- WITHOUT ---
$ ./server -p 9090 --naive-recv
  $ ./client --mode fragment       (1 request as 8 one-byte segments)
  23:35:44.049 [client:26658] INFO  sending one 8-byte SYSINFO_REQ as 8 separate 1-byte segments, 100 ms apart
  23:35:44.050 [client:26658] ERROR server error 3: incomplete message
  RESULT: the split request was NOT understood (0.00 s)
  $ ./client --mode pipeline -q    (5 requests glued into one write)
  23:35:44.057 [client:26660] INFO  sending 5 SYSINFO_REQs glued together in one write() (40 bytes)
  23:35:47.210 [client:26660] ERROR receive failed: socket timeout
  RESULT: 1/5 replies - 4 glued request(s) were lost
server log:
  23:35:44.050 [server:26659] WARN  127.0.0.1:44248: naive recv: one read() returned 1 byte(s), less than the 8-byte header -> taken as a broken message
  23:35:44.057 [server:26661] WARN  127.0.0.1:44260: naive recv: one read() returned 40 bytes; parsed 1 message, the other 32 byte(s) are discarded (requests lost)
  23:35:47.212 [server:26661] INFO  127.0.0.1:44260: client closed the connection after 1 request(s)

--- WITH ---
$ ./server -p 9090 
  $ ./client --mode fragment       (1 request as 8 one-byte segments)
  23:35:47.359 [client:26677] INFO  sending one 8-byte SYSINFO_REQ as 8 separate 1-byte segments, 100 ms apart
  RESULT: the 8 pieces were reassembled into one request; reply from server pid 26678 after 0.70 s
  $ ./client --mode pipeline -q    (5 requests glued into one write)
  23:35:48.073 [client:26681] INFO  sending 5 SYSINFO_REQs glued together in one write() (40 bytes)
  RESULT: 5/5 replies - every glued request was answered
server log:
  23:35:48.064 [server:26678] INFO  127.0.0.1:44276: client closed the connection after 1 request(s)
  23:35:48.075 [server:26682] INFO  127.0.0.1:44292: client closed the connection after 5 request(s)

--- Checks [6] ---
  [PASS] without: the split request is rejected, 4 of 5 glued requests lost
  [PASS] with: the split request is reassembled, 5/5 glued requests answered

